            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating peer confidence**\n\nI’m thinking about how each analyst's reported confidence might indicate their confidence in their recommendations. If they each initially observe three distinct surveys, this makes things tricky since the confidence value is potentially not straightforward. It seems like we should treat these peer forecasts as mere probability estimates rather than absolute certainties, possibly averaging them. The question revolves around how to benchmark these forecasts against Bayesian social information and peer reports, so aggregating all forecasts could be beneficial.**Analyzing peer reports**\n\nI'm contemplating whether the hidden structure of peer reports can help identify survey observations. We only have our source IDs, lacking peer survey details, which could weaken the aggregate evidence if recommendations rely on three observations. It seems like I should calculate report likelihood instead of just counting observations. Confidence might help gauge the strength of these reports. If A's confidence is .6857, could it suggest something about its own posterior? It's possible the analyst’s confidence isn't truly Bayesian.",
